# Operator credentials for `make provision` and `make cloudflare-apply` (D8).
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env    # then fill deploy/.env in
#
# `deploy/.env` is gitignored and must stay that way -- a test asserts it. Fill in the COPY,
# never this file: this one is committed, and a value typed here ships to everyone.
# Anything already exported in your shell takes precedence over what is written here.
#
# These are needed for provisioning and for edge configuration only. Deploying does NOT use
# them: that is `promote.yml`, a workflow dispatch that re-tags the image in the registry,
# and the box's own timer notices within 30s. Nothing here belongs in GitHub Secrets.

# --- Hetzner (make provision) -----------------------------------------------------------
# The tunnel's credential, baked into cloud-init so cloudflared can dial out.
# Cloudflare dashboard > Zero Trust > Networks > Tunnels > (tunnel) > Configure.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# --- Cloudflare (make cloudflare-apply) -------------------------------------------------
# API token, NOT the global API key. Create at My Profile > API Tokens. Three permissions,
# one per call the script makes -- named as the dashboard names them:
#
#   Zone   > Cache Rules       > Edit   -> PUT rulesets/phases/http_request_cache_settings
#   Zone   > Zone WAF          > Edit   -> PUT rulesets/phases/http_ratelimit
#   Account > Cloudflare Tunnel > Edit   -> PUT cfd_tunnel/<id>/configurations
#
# There is NO "Rate Limit" permission at zone scope. Rate limiting rules are a WAF product
# deployed through the ruleset engine, so Zone WAF is what authorises the http_ratelimit
# phase. A token short a permission fails loudly -- cloudflare-apply.sh prints the API's own
# .errors and exits non-zero -- so a wrong scope is caught on the first apply, never silently.
# This token can rewrite the zone's rulesets; treat it accordingly.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Zone ID for shipman.dev -- dashboard overview page, right-hand column.
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=

# Account ID -- same column, directly beneath the zone ID.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Tunnel UUID (the tunnel's own id, not its token) -- Zero Trust > Networks > Tunnels.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_ID=

# --- Optional -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults are `upgauge` and `debian-13`; both are only worth setting for a throwaway probe
# box. Provisioning is keyed on the server NAME, so a second name is a second ~$6.49/mo box.
# UPGAUGE_SERVER_NAME=
# UPGAUGE_OS_IMAGE=
